WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.100
Good morning, Hank, it’s Tuesday.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.020
So Yesterday, Sarah and I flew to New York
City.

00:00:03.029 --> 00:00:06.930
The descent was just a bit bumpy, but we landed
safely, drove into the city, and met up with

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.320
our best friends, Chris and Marina.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.440
Chris: Good morning, America.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.780
I was wearing my fancy shoes because we were
in town for the premiere of the movie Miss

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.030
Peregrine’s Home for Peculiar Children.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.710
Got a delicious lunch of sushi, where I irrevocably
stained my dress shirt, so we headed over

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.610
to a department store, where there were too
many ties to choose from.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.230
If it looks like I hate trying on clothes,
that’s because I do.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.810
But I eventually found a suitable shirt, and
then put the stained shirt back on so as not

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.020
to stain the new one, and went for a walk.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.980
I love walking in New York, even in uncomfortable
shoes, because you’re both alone and not

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.500
in a way I find deeply satisfying, even peaceful.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.070
I also love catching snippets of strangers’
conversations.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.390
“Don’t you have to like someone to love
them?” one young person said to another

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:47.720
as they walked past me.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.520
We walked through an outdoor bookstore, where
I sneakily signed some copies of Looking for Alaska

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.079
And then on my way to the hotel, I happened
across a Crash Course fan.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:55.079
John: What’s your name again?

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:56.079
Kerry: Kerry

00:00:56.079 --> 00:00:57.450
John: And then I ran into Kerry!

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.540
Kerry: Big fan.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.540
John’s awesome

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:03.491
We checked into our aggressively gold hotel
room, I ironed my new shirts, suited up, and

00:01:03.491 --> 00:01:04.491
then got in the elevator.

00:01:04.491 --> 00:01:05.650
I don’t like this tie.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.500
I then met up with Ransom Riggs, the author
of Miss Peregrine.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.500
John: It’s all voiceover—

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.500
Ransom: Oh

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:11.500
John: so it doesn’t really matter what you
say

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.880
I’ve known Ransom for half my life.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.150
Like, when we were in college together, we
took a road trip to the Grand Canyon with

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.150
our friend Kathy.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.710
Are you taking pictures of me, dude?

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.110
We read James Joyce together and wrote comedy
sketches together, and I was always in awe

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.710
of Ransom’s imagination.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:29.630
I think ultimately my favorite feeling isn’t
joy or excitement, but wonder.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.670
The wonder I feel when walking through an
autumn forest with my kid who’s never before

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:37.880
seen the leaves turn color, or the wonder
I feel when in the company of a great book

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:38.920
or movie.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.850
Hanging out Ransom always brings me that kind
of wonder because he has such a particular

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:47.470
and interesting way of looking at the world,
and that’s also how I feel about his books.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.560
They wonderful, in the original sense of the
word.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.610
After hanging out for a while, I went back
to my room

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:53.610
Changed my tie

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.610
And then met up with Sarah

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.460
John: I don’t look as nice as you do, but
I look all right.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.680
Sarah: No, you look awesome! I love it.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.900
And then my publisher, Julie Strauss-Gable,
dropped by, and we all headed to the movie

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:02.109
theatre for the screening.

00:02:02.109 --> 00:02:05.860
Before the movie, Ransom and his wife, the
brilliant novelist Tahereh Mafi, posed on

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.440
the red carpet, and I snuck back to be among
the photographers.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.370
And then I took my seat, where I caught a
magikarp.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.601
Now we shall cut from the magikarp to me turning
on the camera after the move ended.

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.069
You can’t see me but I just saw the movie
and it’s so good!

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:21.240
Miss Peregine made me feel the way Edward
Scissorhands did decades ago.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.280
It was exciting and peculiar, and explored
with great wisdom the tension between the

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.720
past you did have, the one you wanted to have,
and the present you find yourself in.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.099
And that’s what I kept thinking about as
I watched Ransom and Tahereh on the red carpet

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:35.819
after the movie.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.860
Like, if you told me twenty years ago that
this day would happen, I would have thought,

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.120
“That’s gonna be so cool, going to a movie
premiere, all dressed up, people taking our

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.070
pictures while we hang out with movie stars."

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:47.370
And it is cool.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:51.700
Ransom and I have both been astonishingly
lucky in our professional lives.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:56.290
But the really wonderful part of yesterday
for me wasn’t the movie stars or the red carpet.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:01.300
It was seeing a great movie with my wife and
dear friends, one of whom has known me since

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.790
I was a kid on the edge of the Grand Canyon,
before I’d even learned to fear heights.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:11.030
In the end, I think people make experiences
wonderful, not the other way around.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.560
Hank, I’ll see you on Friday,

